29
00:01:44,761 --> 00:01:47,760
But before there was Pong, there was the video game

30
00:01:47,761 --> 00:01:52,760
Ping Pong, the brainchild of television engineer Ralph Baer.

31
00:01:52,761 --> 00:01:53,760
Oops.

32
00:01:53,761 --> 00:01:56,760
It all began in 1966.

33
00:01:56,860 --> 00:01:58,760
While employed by a defense contractor,

34
00:01:58,860 --> 00:02:01,760
Baer came up with the idea of a device that would allow people

35
00:02:01,860 --> 00:02:03,760
to play games using their television sets.

36
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:09,300
The motivation was very simple.

37
00:02:09,400 --> 00:02:13,120
40 million TV sets are out there in the US alone.

38
00:02:13,220 --> 00:02:16,220
If you attach something to 1% of those sets,

39
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:18,460
that's 400,000 units.

40
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,560
Multiply that by a sales price of 25 bucks,

41
00:02:21,561 --> 00:02:25,400
That's $100 million worth of stuff.

42
00:02:25,500 --> 00:02:27,960
Within two years, Baer and several associates

43
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:30,860
completed construction of a prototype.

44
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:32,500
They called it the Brown Box.

45
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:38,220
Like the original Brown Box, this replica

46
