equivalent to a one-page Microsoft Word document.

48
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:49,760
But that's enough brain power to generate simple shapes.

49
00:02:49,761 --> 00:02:52,800
In 1968, using these simple shapes,

50
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:55,760
Bayer's team came up with an idea destined to help create

51
00:02:55,860 --> 00:02:58,760
an entertainment renaissance.

52
00:02:58,860 --> 00:03:00,900
Let's have a machine control spot,

53
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,900
a spot that's moved by some logic in the circuitry.

54
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:07,620
The instant we had the spot moving back and forth,

55
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:09,620
we knew we had something.

56
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:13,320
And that something evolved into ping pong.

57
00:03:13,420 --> 00:03:16,420
This rare 1967 footage featuring Ralph Bayer

58
00:03:16,520 --> 00:03:19,420
is one of the earliest recordings of a video game.

59
00:03:19,421 --> 00:03:24,420
Here we go. Up and down and up.

60
00:03:24,520 --> 00:03:28,420
He's getting tricky. Missed it.

61
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:33,420
One up. A series of circuits

62
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:36,420
